diff --git a/docs/tier1_env_vars.md b/docs/tier1_env_vars.md
index 21176b267..d51fa537c 100644
--- a/docs/tier1_env_vars.md
+++ b/docs/tier1_env_vars.md
@@ -773,2 +783,2 @@ lights should remain visible at every angle instead of blinking out.
-- `MC2_TERRAIN_SHORELINE=1` — land-side wet/foam shoreline band (TERRAIN-SHORELINE-V3), placed by **elevation** in the frag (`v_worldPos.z` vs `u_waterElevation` — the same source the water fast path uses) so the band hugs the RENDERED waterline by construction, at any LOD/slope. No sidecar is required. Default **ON** since SHORELINE-SMOOTHING-REVISIT-1 (deferred-sweep); `=0` turns the terrain-side band off. **CORRECTED by SHORELINE-GLOW-1** — this row previously claimed two things that were not true. (a) It said the gate "suppresses the legacy screen-space `runShoreline()` post pass whenever this gate is on". It did not: the suppression's only input, `gos_TerrainLodChunk_IsShorelineMaskActive()`, was a third reader of this variable that kept the pre-flip default-OFF idiom, so on a stock launch the V3 band and the legacy pass both ran. All readers now share one accessor, and the suppression itself is opt-in behind `MC2_SHORELINE_LEGACY_SUPPRESS` (below). (b) It described the legacy pass as tracing "the tile-quantized water edge". It cannot reach water at all — see `MC2_SHORELINE_LEGACY_SUPPRESS` for the shader-by-shader reason.
-- `MC2_SHORELINE_LEGACY_SUPPRESS=1` — SHORELINE-GLOW-1. Opt in to letting the active V3 shoreline band actually suppress the legacy screen-space `runShoreline()` post pass. Default **OFF**, so stock is byte-identical to the shipped build. **What the legacy pass actually does, since every prior note about it was wrong:** `shaders/shoreline.frag:22` picks its pixels with `GBuffer1.a > 0.15 && < 0.35`, and on the production path nothing water-like is in that band — the MDI water frag (`gos_terrain_water_mdi.frag`) and the non-MDI water fast-path frag (`gos_tex_vertex.frag`) both write alpha **0.0**, `terrain_lod_chunk.frag` writes alpha **1.0**, and the MRT1 clear is **0.0**. A census of every fragment shader writing attachment 1 leaves only `static_prop.frag` (0.25 solid prop, 0.1875 alpha-test lattice) inside the band, plus `gos_terrain.frag`'s continuous `materialAlpha` — which belongs to the legacy terrain shader and is not drawing while `MC2_TERRAIN_LOD_CHUNK` is default-ON. So the pass applies an animated fbm foam brighten (up to 1.4× multiplicative) to **static-prop pixels within ~6 screen texels of terrain** — a shimmering rim where buildings and fences meet the ground. Setting this to 1 removes that rim and changes no water pixel. Kept opt-in because it removes brightness from prop edges while a dark-props report is open, and no capture has graded it.
+- `MC2_TERRAIN_SHORELINE=1` — land-side wet/foam shoreline band (TERRAIN-SHORELINE-V3), placed by **elevation** in the frag (`v_worldPos.z` vs `u_waterElevation` — the same source the water fast path uses) so the band hugs the RENDERED waterline by construction, at any LOD/slope. No sidecar is required. Default **ON** since SHORELINE-SMOOTHING-REVISIT-1 (deferred-sweep); `=0` turns the terrain-side band off. **CORRECTED by SHORELINE-GLOW-1** — this row previously claimed two things that were not true. (a) It said the gate "suppresses the legacy screen-space `runShoreline()` post pass whenever this gate is on". It did not: the suppression's only input, `gos_TerrainLodChunk_IsShorelineMaskActive()`, was a third reader of this variable that kept the pre-flip default-OFF idiom, so on a stock launch the V3 band and the legacy pass both ran. All readers now share one accessor. *(Updated by LEGACY-SHORELINE-MISAIM-1: the suppression is no longer opt-in **and no longer keyed on this variable at all** — it is `MC2_SHORELINE_LEGACY_SUPPRESS` alone, default ON. Setting `MC2_TERRAIN_SHORELINE=0` therefore no longer brings the legacy pass back.)* (b) It described the legacy pass as tracing "the tile-quantized water edge". It cannot reach water at all — see `MC2_SHORELINE_LEGACY_SUPPRESS` for the shader-by-shader reason.
+- `MC2_SHORELINE_LEGACY_SUPPRESS=0` — **DEFAULT FLIPPED OFF → ON by LEGACY-SHORELINE-MISAIM-1 (2026-07-31).** The legacy screen-space `runShoreline()` post pass is now **suppressed on a default launch**; `=0` is the only revert token and restores it exactly as it shipped. This row previously read "`=1` … Default **OFF**, so stock is byte-identical to the shipped build" — that is no longer true and the sentence is replaced rather than left standing. **Why the flip, against SHORELINE-GLOW-1's own explicit "kept opt-in" note:** the brightness being removed is *animated* — `shoreline.frag` scrolls its fbm by `time` and pulses it with `sin(time*2.0)` — so the shipped behaviour is a shimmer crawling along the silhouette of a **static building**. That is not a brightness judgement call; no grading pass would have kept it. Holding a mis-aimed pass on by default while waiting for a capture that this lane cannot take is the bargain that produced the incapable-fix list this flip comes from. **The pass is NOT deleted, and this gate is not a no-op:** `setShorelineSuppressedByTerrainMask()` is still called only from the LOD-chunk terrain draw, so under `MC2_TERRAIN_LOD_CHUNK=0` the legacy terrain shader draws, its continuous `materialAlpha` *does* land in the 0.15–0.35 band, and the pass still runs and still foams water — the one configuration it was written for. **Also changed:** the suppression no longer ANDs with `MC2_TERRAIN_SHORELINE`; that coupling meant turning the V3 band off silently restored the prop rim. **Flip-back A/B:** any mission with buildings or fences near terrain (mc2_01, mc2_03) with a **moving** camera — the artifact is temporal and a single still frame understates it. **What the legacy pass actually does, since every prior note about it was wrong:** `shaders/shoreline.frag:22` picks its pixels with `GBuffer1.a > 0.15 && < 0.35`, and on the production path nothing water-like is in that band — the MDI water frag (`gos_terrain_water_mdi.frag`) and the non-MDI water fast-path frag (`gos_tex_vertex.frag`) both write alpha **0.0**, `terrain_lod_chunk.frag` writes alpha **1.0**, and the MRT1 clear is **0.0**. A census of every fragment shader writing attachment 1 leaves only `static_prop.frag` (0.25 solid prop, 0.1875 alpha-test lattice) inside the band, plus `gos_terrain.frag`'s continuous `materialAlpha` — which belongs to the legacy terrain shader and is not drawing while `MC2_TERRAIN_LOD_CHUNK` is default-ON. So the pass applies an animated fbm foam brighten (up to 1.4× multiplicative) to **static-prop pixels within ~6 screen texels of terrain** — a shimmering rim where buildings and fences meet the ground. Suppressing it removes that rim and changes no water pixel. *(Original SHORELINE-GLOW-1 rationale, preserved so the reversal can be judged against it: "Kept opt-in because it removes brightness from prop edges while a dark-props report is open, and no capture has graded it." Both facts are still true; see the flip argument above for why they no longer carry.)*
